detailedExplanation:
### Student-Friendly Deep Dive: Promises & The Promises/A+ Standard

#### 1. Why Did JavaScript Need Promises? (The Old Problem)
Before ES6 (2015), JavaScript handled all background tasks using callbacks.
For example, if you wanted to fetch a user, then fetch their orders, then fetch order details, you had to write callbacks inside callbacks inside callbacks:

// The Old Nightmare: Callback Hell (Pyramid of Doom)
getUser(userId, function(user) {
  getOrders(user.id, function(orders) {
    getOrderDetails(orders[0].id, function(details) {
      console.log(details);
    });
  });
});

This caused two major headaches for developers:
1. Callback Hell: The code looks like a giant sideways pyramid (>), very hard to read and debug.
2. Inversion of Control: You give your callback function to another library, hoping they call it once. But what if they call it twice? Or never call it?

#### 2. How Promises Solve This Completely
A Promise turns asynchronous code into clean, top-to-bottom readable code:

getUser(userId)
  .then(user => getOrders(user.id))
  .then(orders => getOrderDetails(orders[0].id))
  .then(details => console.log(details))
  .catch(error => console.error("Error occurred:", error))
  .finally(() => console.log("Done loading"));

#### 3. The 3 States & Internal Engine Rules
Every Promise object in JavaScript has two hidden internal properties:
- [[PromiseState]]: Starts as 'pending'. Changes to 'fulfilled' on resolve, or 'rejected' on reject.
- [[PromiseResult]]: Starts as undefined. Holds your data when resolved, or the Error object when rejected.

Golden Rule of Settled Promises:
Once a Promise moves from pending to fulfilled or rejected, it is called Settled.
Settled promises are immutable—you cannot change their value, and you cannot resolve or reject them again.

#### 4. The Microtask Queue (Event Loop Timing)
Have you ever wondered why console.log('End') prints before promise.then()?
Here is the exact execution order in JavaScript:
1. Synchronous Call Stack: JavaScript runs your normal lines of code first (Line 1, Line 2, etc.).
2. Microtask Queue (VIP line): Promise callbacks (.then, .catch, .finally) go here! The moment the call stack is empty, JavaScript executes ALL microtasks immediately.
3. Macrotask Queue (Regular line): setTimeout, setInterval, and DOM events go here. They only run after all microtasks are finished!

realWorldExample:
Real-World Practical Example: Fetching Weather Data with Loading Spinner and Error Handling

Imagine a weather app on your mobile screen:
1. When user clicks 'Search', turn on loading spinner.
2. Call weather API using a Promise.
3. If success (.then): Show temperature and city name.
4. If failed (.catch): Show 'City not found or Network Error' message.
5. In all cases (.finally): Turn off loading spinner.

function fetchCityWeather(cityName) {
  // Show spinner
  showLoadingSpinner(true);

  fetch(`https://api.weather.com/v1/${cityName}`)
    .then(response => {
      if (!response.ok) {
        throw new Error("City not found or server error");
      }
      return response.json(); // Parses JSON and returns a new promise
    })
    .then(weatherData => {
      displayWeather(weatherData);
    })
    .catch(error => {
      showErrorMessage(error.message);
    })
    .finally(() => {
      // Always hide spinner, whether success or error!
      showLoadingSpinner(false);
    });
}

